{
  "spec": "Create a top-level module that instantiates the provided mod_a module exactly once. The mod_a module has two inputs (in1, in2) and one output (out) and implements a non-commutative function (in1 AND NOT in2). Connect the top_module's inputs a and b to mod_a's in1 and in2 respectively, and connect mod_a's out to top_module's out. Do not add any extra logic.",
  "given_sv": "module mod_a(input in1, input in2, output out);\n  assign out = in1 & ~in2;\nendmodule",
  "ref_sv": "module top_module(input a, input b, output out);\n  assign out = a & ~b;\nendmodule",
  "solution_sv": "module top_module(input a, input b, output out);\n  mod_a u_mod_a (\n    .in1(a),\n    .in2(b),\n    .out(out)\n  );\nendmodule",
  "directed": [
    {"a": 1, "b": 0},
    {"a": 0, "b": 1},
    {"a": 1, "b": 1},
    {"a": 0, "b": 0}
  ],
  "hints": {
    "L0": "Think about how to connect the two inputs of the top module to the two inputs of the provided module, noting that the provided module's function is not symmetric.",
    "L3": "Connect top_module's a to mod_a's in1, top_module's b to mod_a's in2, and mod_a's out to top_module's out."
  },
  "common_mistakes": [
    "Swapping the connections of a and b to in1 and in2, which changes the output because mod_a is non-commutative.",
    "Forgetting to connect the output of mod_a to top_module's out.",
    "Instantiating mod_a more than once or not at all."
  ]
}

